<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-amplitude" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Distance from Equilibrium</title>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       INTRO + INTERACTIVE
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    We are not quite done exploring the behavior of solutions to the free undamped model! 
    We know the mass oscillates forever. In this page we explore how far from
    equilibrium it actually gets. Watch the spring below, and pay attention
    to when it's at its most stretched and most compressed! How does this connect to what's happening in the graph on the right?
  </p>

  <figure xml:id="fig-spring-amplitude">
    <caption>
      The solution <m>x(t) = 2\cos(3t)</m>. The dashed horizontal lines on
      the graph mark the maximum and minimum values. Notice these match the
      "max stretched" and "max compressed" moments on the spring.
    </caption>
    <interactive xml:id="interactive-spring-amplitude"
      iframe="ch3-graph/interactive-spring-amplitude.html"
      width="100%" aspect="5:3">
      <description>
        An animated spring and position graph for x of t equals 2 cosine of
        3t. When the block reaches maximum stretch (x equals 2), the block
        darkens and a label reads "max stretched, distance from equilibrium
        equals 2." When it reaches maximum compression (x equals negative 2),
        a similar label appears. Dashed horizontal lines on the graph at y
        equals 2 and y equals negative 2 are labeled "distance from
        equilibrium equals 2."
      </description>
    </interactive>
  </figure>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       MC CHECKPOINT
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mc-amplitude-2cos3t">
    <statement>
      <p>
        From the graph above, what is the furthest distance the mass
        ever gets from equilibrium?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="yes">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement><p><m>2</m></p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Right: the graph peaks at <m>x = 2</m> and bottoms out at
            <m>x = -2</m>, so the mass never gets more than 2 units from
            equilibrium. <m>\checkmark</m>
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement><p><m>3</m></p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            The 3 is <m>\omega_0</m>, the frequency: it controls how fast
            the mass oscillates, not how far it goes. Look at the graph: the
            curve never reaches <m>y = 3</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement><p><m>4</m></p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            The mass swings between <m>x = -2</m> and <m>x = 2</m>, so the
            total distance from one extreme to the other is 4, but the
            question asks for distance from <em>equilibrium</em>, which is
            just half of that.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement><p><m>\dfrac{2\pi}{3}</m></p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            That's the period of <m>x(t) = 2\cos(3t)</m>
            (<m>T = 2\pi/\omega_0 = 2\pi/3</m>), a time, not a distance.
            Look at the height of the curve on the graph for the maximum distance.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       AMPLITUDE OBSERVATION
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    That maximum distance is just the amplitude of the cosine function, which equals the
    coefficient out front. In general, for any solution of the form
    <m>x(t) = c_1\cos(\omega_0 t) + c_2\sin(\omega_0 t)</m>, the amplitude is
    <m>A = \sqrt{c_1^2 + c_2^2}</m>.
  </p>

  <observation xml:id="obs-amplitude">
    <title>Maximum Distance from Equilibrium</title>
    <p>
      If <m>x(t) = c_1\cos(\omega_0 t) + c_2\sin(\omega_0 t)</m> is the
      position of a mass in a spring-mass system, then the maximum distance
      the mass ever gets from equilibrium is the <term>amplitude</term>:
      <me>A = \sqrt{c_1^2 + c_2^2}.</me>
    </p>
  </observation>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       EXAMPLE: y'' + 4y = 0, y(0)=-3, y'(0)=0
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    Let's put all of this together with an example. We will be exploring an additional question at the end regarding the speed
     of the spring.
  </p>

  <example xml:id="ex-spring-amplitude-speed">
    <title>A Mass-Spring System: Amplitude and Speed</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Suppose a mass-spring system is described by
        <me>y'' + 4y = 0, \qquad y(0) = -3, \quad y'(0) = 0.</me>
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <task xml:id="task-amplitude-solve">
      <title>Step 1: Solve the IVP</title>
      <statement><p>Find <m>y(t)</m>.</p></statement>
     <hint>
  <p>
    This is in the form of <xref ref="obs-simple-harmonic-ode"/>: identify
    <m>\omega</m> and write down the general solution, then apply the initial conditions.
  </p>
</hint>
<solution>
  <p>
    The equation <m>y'' + 4y = 0</m> is in the form of <xref ref="obs-simple-harmonic-ode"/>
    with <m>\omega = 2</m>, so the general solution is:
    <me>y(t) = c_1\cos(2t) + c_2\sin(2t).</me>
    Applying initial conditions:
    <md>
      <mrow>y(0) = c_1 \amp= -3,</mrow>
      <mrow>y'(0) = 2c_2 \amp= 0 \implies c_2 = 0.</mrow>
    </md>
    So the solution is:
    <me>y(t) = -3\cos(2t).</me>
  </p>
</solution>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="task-amplitude-distance">
      <title>Step 2: How far from equilibrium?</title>
      <statement><p>What is the furthest the mass gets from equilibrium?</p></statement>
      <hint>
        <p>Identify the amplitude <m>A = \sqrt{c_1^2 + c_2^2}</m>.</p>
      </hint>
      <solution>
        <p>
          With <m>c_1 = -3</m> and <m>c_2 = 0</m>:
          <me>A = \sqrt{(-3)^2 + 0^2} = 3.</me>
          The mass gets at most <m>3</m> units from equilibrium. We can also
          see this directly: <m>y(t) = -3\cos(2t)</m> has maximum value 3
          and minimum value <m>-3</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="task-amplitude-speed">
      <title>Step 3: Does it slow down over time?</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Will the mass move slower as it passes through equilibrium the
          5th time compared to the 1st time?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
  <p>
    <ol>
      <li><p>Compute <m>y'(t)</m>.</p></li>
      <li><p>
        Find all times <m>t^*</m> when the mass passes through
        equilibrium, i.e. solve <m>y(t^*) = 0</m>.
      </p></li>
      <li><p>
        Evaluate the speed <m>|y'(t^*)|</m> at those times and compare.
      </p></li>
    </ol>
  </p>
</hint>
<solution>
  <p>
    <alert>1.</alert> Compute <m>y'(t)</m>:
    <me>y'(t) = 6\sin(2t).</me>
  </p>
  <p>
    <alert>2.</alert> The mass passes through equilibrium when <m>y(t^*) = 0</m>, i.e.
    <m>-3\cos(2t^*) = 0</m>, so <m>\cos(2t^*) = 0</m>, giving:
    <me>t^* = \frac{\pi}{4} + \frac{n\pi}{2}, \qquad n = 0, 1, 2, \ldots</me>
  </p>
  <p>
    <alert>3.</alert> Evaluate the speed at each crossing:
    <me>|y'(t^*)| = |6\sin(2t^*)| = \left|6\sin\!\left(\frac{\pi}{2} + n\pi\right)\right|.</me>
    Since <m>\sin(\pi/2 + n\pi) = \pm 1</m> for all integers <m>n</m>,
    we get:
    <me>|y'(t^*)| = 6 \quad \text{every time.}</me>
    The speed at equilibrium is always exactly 6, whether it's the
    1st crossing or the 5th. Free undamped motion has constant
    amplitude: no energy is lost, so the mass moves just as fast
    through equilibrium every time.
  </p>
</solution>
    </task>

  </example>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       KEY TAKEAWAYS
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<p>
  So there we have it. From the previous pages, we learned how to interpret and determine the frequency and period of mass.
   Then we explored how it goes back and forth through the equilibrium position infinitely many times as time goes on forever. 
And now, we understand how to connect the amplitude of <m>x(t)</m> with the distance the spring is from the equilibrium. 
We also explored the slightly more subtle point that the speed at which it passes through equilibrium never changes.
This should be clear from the graph though, as <m>x(t)</m> is a periodic function that keeps repeating the same motion forever!
</p>
  <conclusion>
    <title>Key Takeaways</title>
    <p><ul>
      <li><p>
        <alert>Amplitude:</alert> the maximum distance from equilibrium is
        <m>A = \sqrt{c_1^2 + c_2^2}</m>. For a pure cosine or sine solution,
        it's just the coefficient out front.
      </p></li>
      <li><p>
        <alert>Speed at equilibrium is constant:</alert> since there's no
        damping, no energy is lost. The mass passes through equilibrium at
        the same speed every time.
      </p></li>
      <li><p>
        <alert>Free undamped motion is perfectly periodic:</alert> same
        amplitude, same speed, same period, forever! Damping changes all
        of this, as we'll see next.
      </p></li>
    </ul></p>
  </conclusion>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       NO VIDEO + QUESTION FORM
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

